<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Fort Wayne (1809)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1809</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Fort Wayne of 30 September 1809 was signed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a>, governor of Indiana Territory, and leaders of the Delaware, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a>, Miami and Eel River peoples. It ceded about three million acres of land in present-day Indiana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> denounced it, arguing that no single nation could sell land held in common, and it fed the conflict that led to Tippecanoe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fort-wayne-1809.jpg" alt="Treaty of Fort Wayne (1809)" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArnoldPlaton, vector version of User:Charles_Edward's Indiana Indian treaties.jpg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indiana_Indian_treaties.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
